metadata.detail:
FOUND by running `boss gate` by hand with no KUBECONFIG, 2026-08-29.

THE DEFECT, at crates/orchestrators/boss-cli/src/gate.rs:439:

    let running = running_gates(namespace).unwrap_or(0);
    if shared && running > 0 { bail!(...) }

`running_gates` is careful. It bails on a failed `kubectl get pods` and carries the exact diagnostic a stuck operator needs — 'kubectl get pods — is KUBECONFIG set and the cluster reachable?' (gate.rs:358). The call site throws that away. Any error at all — no KUBECONFIG, unreachable API server, a service account that may create Jobs but not list pods — becomes the number ZERO, which is indistinguishable from 'the cluster is healthy and idle'.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A BAD ERROR MESSAGE. Zero is precisely the value that satisfies the guard. `if shared && running > 0` is the check protecting a runner whose /gate-target is a shared PVC, and its own bail! text says what it is protecting against: 'Two gates on one disk cross their receipts — on 2026-08-24 a receipt naming one branch's head was reported under another, and all three results were discarded.' So the one condition that must never be guessed is guessed, and guessed in the permissive direction. The guard fails OPEN.

It did not bite today only by luck: the same missing KUBECONFIG that blinded the check also made the subsequent `kubectl create` fail, so nothing was launched. An environment where the SA can CREATE Jobs but not LIST pods — a plausible least-privilege setup, and exactly the shape of /etc/boss-dev/kubeconfig, which is scoped enough that it cannot list nodes — would sail past the guard and launch a second gate onto the shared disk.

SECOND SYMPTOM, same line: because the diagnostic is discarded, the operator sees a raw kubectl openapi error ('failed to download openapi: dial tcp 127.0.0.1:8080: connection refused') from the LATER create call, instead of the message written for this exact case. I spent a round trip on it. The good error existed the whole time.

THE FIX, small and true: an error is not zero, and what to do about it depends on the workspace.

    let running = match running_gates(namespace) {
        Ok(n) => n,
        // A shared workspace is the case the guard exists for. If we
        // cannot verify the disk is free, refuse — never assume.
        Err(e) if shared => return Err(e).context(
            "cannot verify whether another gate is running, and this runner mounts a \
             SHARED /gate-target"),
        // Own workspace: the count only drives an advisory notice.
        Err(_) => 0,
    };

That keeps the permissive path exactly where it is harmless (the `!shared` branch only prints a crowding notice) and makes the protective path fail closed, matching how the merge checks already behave.

WORTH SAYING PLAINLY: this is the same shape as the reliability work filed today. A check that cannot run returns a value that reads as 'fine' rather than 'unknown' — the same failure as `total: 0` from a wrong deployment, an `inactive` systemd unit that does not exist, and `last-firing: null` from an empty table. CLAUDE.md already names it: 'a wrong target answers instead of erroring'. Here the wrong answer defeats a safety guard rather than merely misleading a reader.

RELATED, and NOT fixed here: `boss gate` run by hand needs KUBECONFIG=/etc/boss-dev/kubeconfig and BOSS_JOBS_URL, and nothing in the verb's help or the publish hand-off says so — `boss publish` ends with 'next — boss gate <branch> --wait (run it on boss-gcp...)', which is where I ran it, and it still failed. The doors list in CLAUDE.md should carry the environment a hand-run gate needs, since a verb run by hand inherits no systemd unit.
